WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.173
Writing the rubric.

2
00:00:01.453 --> 00:00:05.272
A judge prompt is a rubric plus the material to apply it to.

3
00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:08.240
Rubrics that calibrate well share a few properties.

4
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.187
State the criterion as checkable conditions.

5
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:22.560
Not “the reply is accurate,” but “every policy claim in the reply (time limits, fees, eligibility, exceptions, refund methods) is supported by the policy excerpt.

6
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:31.821
” Anthropic’s tips give an example of exactly this kind: an answer must mention a specific name in its first sentence, or it’s graded incorrect.

7
00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:33.381
Say what doesn’t count.

8
00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:40.296
If length and tone are irrelevant, say so, because judges reward them otherwise (the verbosity bias below).

9
00:00:40.576 --> 00:00:43.520
Cover the edge cases you found in error analysis.

10
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.955
What should happen when the policy is silent?

11
00:00:46.235 --> 00:00:48.432
When the reply is accurate but evasive?

12
00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:50.440
Write the answer into the rubric.

13
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.171
Give the judge a way out.

14
00:00:52.451 --> 00:01:01.432
Anthropic’s agent-evals write-up suggests letting the judge answer “unknown” when it lacks the information to decide, instead of forcing a guess.

15
00:01:01.712 --> 00:01:03.099
Track how often it does.

16
00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:04.979
One criterion per judge.

17
00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:10.208
The same write-up recommends grading each dimension with its own isolated judge.

18
00:01:10.488 --> 00:01:16.525
Separate calls cost more, but each one is simpler, and you get a separate agreement number for each criterion.

19
00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:18.576
Reason first, then decide.

20
00:01:18.856 --> 00:01:23.571
Anthropic’s tips recommend a grader with thinking on, so it reasons before it scores.

21
00:01:23.851 --> 00:01:34.112
With structured outputs you can also order the schema so the critique comes before the verdict: Claude’s docs say required properties keep their schema order in the output.

22
00:01:34.392 --> 00:01:37.187
Expect the rubric to change once you start grading.

23
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:48.709
Shankar and colleagues (2024) call this criteria drift: people need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs is how they discover their criteria.

24
00:01:48.989 --> 00:01:50.291
That isn’t a failure.

25
00:01:50.571 --> 00:01:54.411
It’s why you write the rubric after reading data, and why you revisit it.
